ridiculous in theory and erroneous in fact. The AS. ''thrǣl'', thrall, cannot be derived from ''thyrelian'', ''thyrlian'', thirl (see ''thirl''<sup>1</sup>, ''thrill''<sup>1</sup>), and if it were so derived, it could not mean 'thrilled,' or 'a thrilled man.'] '''I.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A slave; a serf; a bondman; a captive.

{{smaller block|And se thi sone that in seruage<br>For mannis soule was made a ''thralle.''
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|In a dungeon deepe huge nombers lay<br>Of caytive wretched ''thralls'', that wayled night and day.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. v. 45.}}}}

{{smaller block|The actual slave, the ''thrall'', the theow, is found everywhere [in early Britain]. The class is formed and recruited in two ways. The captive taken in war accepts slavery as a lighter doom than death; the freeman who is guilty of certain crimes is degraded to the state of slavery by sentence of law. In either case the servile condition of the parent is inherited by his children.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Encyc. Brit., VIII. 274.}}}}

{{smaller block|The ''thrall'' in person may be free in soul.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

'''2.''' One who is a slave to some desire, appetite, spell, or other influence; one who is in moral bondage.

{{smaller block|Hi ne byeth [they are not] ''threlles'' ne to gold, ne to zeluer, ne to hare caroyne [their flesh], ne to the guodes of fortune.
{{right|''Ayenbite of Inwyt'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 86.}}}}

{{smaller block|The slaves of drink and ''thralls'' of sleep.
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iii. 6. 13.}}}}

'''3.''' Thraldom, literal or figurative; bondage; slavery; subjection.

{{smaller block|The chafed Horse, such ''thrall'' ill-suffering,<br>Begins to snuff, and snort, and leap, and fling.
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas's Weeks, ii., The Handy-Crafts.}}}}
